{{smaller block|The ''assendent'' sothly, as wel in alle natiuitez as in questiouns and elecciouns of tymes, is a thing which that thise Astrologiens gretly obseruen; wher-fore me semeth conuenient sin that I speke of the ''assendent'', to make of it special declaratioun. The ''assendent'' sothly, to take it at the largest, is thilke degree that assendeth at any of thise forseide tymes upon the est Orisonte; and there-fore, gif that any planet assende at that same tyme in thilke forseide degre of his longitude, Men seyn thilke planete is in horoscope.
{{right|''Chaucer.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Sciences that were then in their highest ''ascendant.''
{{right|''Sir W. Temple.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Marlborough had not, when Popery was ''in the ascendant'', crossed himself, shrived himself, done penance, taken the communion in one kind, and, as soon as a turn of fortune came, apostatized back again.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xx.}}}}

'''2.''' Superiority or commanding influence; predominance: especially in the phrase ''to gain the ascendant over'' one.
{{smaller block|<poem>What star I know not, but some star, I find,
Has given thee an ''ascendant'' o’er my mind.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Persius, Satires, v. 66.}}}}
{{smaller block|She had art enough to gain an entire ''ascendant'' over the king.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Voltaire.}}}}
{{smaller block|The secular authority, long unduly depressed, regained the ''ascendant'' with startling rapidity.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Von Ranke.}}}}

'''3.''' An ancestor, or one who precedes in genealogy or degrees of kindred: opposed to ''descendant.''
{{smaller block|The succession of ''ascendants'' of the deceased, of his male paternal ancestors, if any survived him.
{{right|''Maine'', Early Law and Custom, p. 111.}}}}

'''4.''' In ''arch.'', one of the two sides or vertical members of the chambranle of a door or window.
